WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.480
Formatting APIs often look harmless because the program is constructing a string from known pieces.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:14.820
The risk appears when the destination is fixed-size, one input component is longer than expected, or the caller assumes that a formatted buffer is complete without checking the result.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.320
Size-aware formatting is the starting point, not the finish line.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:24.460
When formatting into a buffer, the code should pass the destination capacity and check what the API reports.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.920
If the output was truncated, the program needs a deliberate response.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.980
Sometimes truncation is acceptable for a human-readable status field.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.200
Sometimes it changes the meaning of a path, identifier, or protocol value and must be treated as a failure.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.180
Formatting return values should be checked before the resulting buffer is used.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:50.120
If the operation fails or reports that the output did not fit, later code should not proceed as though the full string exists.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.180
A good migration turns silent assumptions into visible error paths.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.640
Input conversion has a similar shape.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:04.860
scanf-style input needs field widths for string destinations, conversion count checks, and careful handling when input does not match the expected format.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:12.200
Without those checks, the code may accept partial data or leave previous values unchanged while appearing to parse successfully.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:18.180
Numeric conversion should detect invalid input, range errors, leftover characters, and overflow.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:26.560
A robust parser distinguishes a clean value from a value with trailing junk, a value outside the destination type, and a missing value.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:32.940
That distinction matters because accepting partial or ambiguous input can make downstream behavior unpredictable.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.200
Safer formatting and conversion are about contracts.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.340
What format is accepted?

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.080
What size is allowed?

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.900
What does truncation mean?

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.360
What happens when input is malformed?

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.020
The code should answer those questions explicitly so tests and reviewers can verify the behavior.
